Dave,

Friday the 13th is not only superstitious here in the USA, but also in
Europe, where, in fact, the superstition started.  13 has been an
unlucky number in Europe since the dark ages.  Things like the fact that
there are 12 months in the year and 12 hours in the day made 13
"magical", but usually in a bad way.   In the late reniassance, the 13
being unlucky was reinforced by such amazing things like someone
noticing that Christ is the "13th" dinner guest at the "Last Supper". 
Nice, huh?  Also, hangmans nooses always had 13 knots.  This brings us
to the "Friday the 13th" part.  In medieval Europe, Friday was hangmans
day!  Yeah, all of the criminals were hung on Friday.  So, on Friday the
13th, a criminal would walk up the 13 steps of the gallows, then to feel
the 13 knots of the hangmans noose...  yeah, I guess you could call it
unlucky!   

